pedia.org/wiki/Augustus_Pugin"]],"cat":"Nottingham Cathedral","stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"Building began in 1841 on a Catholic church designed by Augustus Pugin, with much of the cost paid by Lord Shrewsbury. It was consecrated in 1844, and in 1852 it became a cathedral.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Cathedral","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Augustus Pugin","note:Augustus Pugin"]],"links":["Augustus Pugin"],"when":"1841","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Cathedral","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Cathedral of St Barnabas"},{"layer":"people","text":"Augustus Pugin, who also designed the interior of the Houses of Parliament, designed the cathedral.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Augustus Pugin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustus_Pugin","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Augustus Pugin","sources":[{"title":"Nottingham Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Cathedral","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Augustus Pugin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustus_Pugin","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Nottingham Cathedral","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Cathedral","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1812","whenSrc":{"title":"Augustus Pugin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustus_Pugin","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1}],"sum":"The Cathedral Church of St. Barnabas is a cathedral of the Roman Catholic Church in the city of Nottingham in Nottinghamshire, England. It is the mother church of the Diocese of Nottingham and seat of the Bishop of Nottingham.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Cathedral","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Cathedral"},"open":[{"what":"place of worship","web":"http://www.stbarnabascathedral.org.uk/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15715,52.95468]},"73960":{"aka":["Standard Court"],"full":"Nottingham General Hospital · Standard Court","y":1781,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":20.2,"levels":"6","osm":"way/168960005","kind":"hospital","qid":"Q7063645","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nottingham%20General%20Hospital.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_General_Hospital","architects":[["John Simpson",null]],"cat":"Nottingham General Hospital","stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"A legacy from the banker John Key paid for a hospital on part of Nottingham Park. Its first building, designed by John Simpson, was begun on 12 February 1781 and opened with 44 beds in September 1782; John Wesley was an early visitor. In 1844 it took in the hurt when a stand collapsed at a hanging on Garner's Hill, killing twelve. Hine added a storey in 1855, and Alfred Waterhouse's round Jubilee Wing opened in 1900. It closed in 1992, its work moved to the Queen's Medical Centre.","source":{"title":"Nottingham General Hospital","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_General_Hospital","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1782","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham General Hospital","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_General_Hospital","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Nottingham General Hospital"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"John Simpson","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/168960005","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"Nottingham General Hospital was a major hospital in Nottingham, England. It was founded in 1781 and closed in 1992.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham General Hospital","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_General_Hospital"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Nottingham Castle","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottingham-castle.html"}],"xy":[-1.15547,52.95189]},"73961":{"aka":["jubilee Wing"],"y":1897,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":20.2,"levels":"6","osm":"way/1244713923","kind":"hospital","architects":[["Alfred Waterhouse","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Waterhouse"]],"stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"A legacy from the banker John Key paid for a hospital on part of Nottingham Park. Its first building, designed by John Simpson, was begun on 12 February 1781 and opened with 44 beds in September 1782; John Wesley was an early visitor. In 1844 it took in the hurt when a stand collapsed at a hanging on Garner's Hill, killing twelve. Hine added a storey in 1855, and Alfred Waterhouse's round Jubilee Wing opened in 1900. It closed in 1992, its work moved to the Queen's Medical Centre.","source":{"title":"Nottingham General Hospital","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_General_Hospital","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1782","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham General Hospital","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_General_Hospital","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Nottingham General Hospital"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"Alfred Waterhouse","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/1244713923","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"The Roundhouse Bar and Kitchen is a pub on Royal Standard Place. The building was built in 1897, according to OpenStreetMap. It was designed by Alfred Waterhouse.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Nottingham Castle","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottingham-castle.html"}],"xy":[-1.15519,52.95172]},"74995":{"today":"The Guildhall","y":1887,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","alts":[{"y":1888,"f":"exact","src":"Wikipedia"},{"y":1888,"f":"exact","src":"Wikidata"}],"h":10,"osm":"way/67922425","kind":"public","listEntry":1246296,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1246296","qid":"Q7063650","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nottingham%20Guildhall%20%282%29.JPG","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall","architects":[["Thomas Verity","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Verity"]],"cat":"Nottingham Guildhall","stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"Built in 1887–88 to designs by Thomas Verity and George Henry Hunt, chosen in a competition judged by Alfred Waterhouse, it replaced the old Guild Hall on Weekday Cross and served as the city's magistrates' court. The council used it as offices until 2010; it has stood largely empty since.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Guildhall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1888","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Guildhall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Guildhall"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"Thomas Verity","source":{"title":"Thomas Verity","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Verity","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"Nottingham Guildhall is a former magistrates' court in Nottingham, England. The structure, which was used by Nottingham City Council as offices in the 1990s, is a Grade II listed building.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Guildhall","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Guildhall"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15068,52.95656]},"75086":{"aka":["Victoria Centre Clock Tower","Nottingham Victoria Clock Tower"],"y":1899,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":38.0,"osm":"way/116816364","listEntry":1270622,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270622","qid":"Q26560657","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Clocktower%20-%20panoramio.jpg","architects":[["Albert Edward Lambert","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Edward_Lambert"]],"cat":"Victoria Centre Clock Tower, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"Nottingham Victoria, the Great Central and Great Northern railways' station, opened on 24 May 1900 and closed on 4 September 1967. It was demolished except for its clock tower, which still stands at the entrance of the Victoria Centre built on the site.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Victoria railway station","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Victoria_railway_station","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Nottingham Victoria station","see":[["Victoria Centre","75088"]]},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"Albert Edward Lambert","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/116816364","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"Nottingham Victoria station: a takeaway. The building was built in 1899, according to OpenStreetMap. It is Grade II listed as Victoria Centre Clock Tower and was designed by Albert Edward Lambert.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.14815,52.95723]},"75088":{"y":1972,"f":"exact","src":"Wikipedia","h":10,"osm":"way/16469518","qid":"Q7926625","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Victoria_Centre_Clock_Tower%2C_Nottingham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8421709.jpg","credit":{"file":"Victoria Centre Clock Tower, Nottingham - geograph.org.uk - 8421709.jpg","year":2026,"author":"Rod Grealish","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"north","text":"A shopping centre opened in 1972 on the site of Nottingham Victoria railway station, whose clock tower still stands beside it.","source":{"title":"Victoria Centre","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria_Centre","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Nottingham Victoria railway station","note:Nottingham Victoria station"]],"links":["Nottingham Victoria station"],"nm":"Victoria Centre","see":[["Nottingham Victoria station","p676"]]}],"sum":"Victoria Centre: a shopping centre on Victoria Centre. The building was built in 1972, according to Wikipedia.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.14751,52.95685]},"76264":{"today":"Arkwright","y":1879,"f":"approx","src":"Wikipedia","h":10.6,"levels":"3","osm":"relation/1750480","kind":"university","listEntry":1255017,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255017","qid":"Q17553866","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Byron%20house%202.JPG","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","cat":"Arkwright Building, Nottingham Trent University","stories":[{"layer":"lacemarket","text":"On the night of 8–9 May 1941 the Luftwaffe bombed Nottingham. Firefighters saved St Mary's Church after an incendiary burned through its roof; three Boots factories, in Poplar Street, Island Street and Station Street, were on fire; University College and the Shakespeare Street chapel were hit. The worst loss was at the Co-op bakery on Meadow Lane, where 49 people died. In all, 159 people were killed that night.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Blitz","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Blitz","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Nottingham Blitz, 1941"},{"layer":"north","text":"Built in 1877–81 for University College Nottingham, with the town's library and a natural history museum, and damaged by bombs in the Second World War. D. H. Lawrence studied here to become a teacher; it is now Nottingham Trent University's Arkwright Building.","source":{"title":"Arkwright Building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["D. H. Lawrence","note:D. H. Lawrence"]],"links":["D. H. Lawrence"],"nm":"University College","see":[["Trent Building","82807"]]},{"layer":"people","text":"Built between 1877 and 1881 for University College Nottingham, where D. H. Lawrence studied during his teacher-training years.[^s1]","source":{"title":"D. H. Lawrence","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._H._Lawrence","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"D. H. Lawrence","sources":[{"title":"Arkwright Building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Arkwright Building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"own":1}],"sum":"The Arkwright Building is a Grade II* listed university building on Shakespeare Street in Nottingham, England. It forms part of the Nottingham Trent University City Campus and is one of the university's principal historic buildings. Built between 1877 and 1881, the building was originally home to University College Nottingham, the city library and a natural history museum.","sumSrc":{"title":"Arkwright Building","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arkwright_Building"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"D. H. Lawrence","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/dh-lawrence.html"}],"xy":[-1.15244,52.95735]}}